[=#firebirdprotocolversion]
= Protocol Version =

When a Firebird client connects, it offers a list of the protocol versions it can speak, and the server picks one.  Each version adds features, so client and server have to agree on which ones are in use.  The Firebird protocol module speaks versions 10 through 13 and defaults to 13.

The //maxprotocolversion// attribute of the listener tag sets the highest protocol version that the server will pick.  Values from 1 to 20 are valid.  Leaving it out, setting it to 0, or setting it to anything above 20 is the same as the default of 13.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-firebirdfeprotocolversion.conf@
}}}
}}}

Raising the maximum above 13 is opt-in.  Versions 14 through 20 add op_ping, batch operations, op_repl_data, statement timeouts, scrollable cursors (op_fetch_scroll and op_info_cursor), and prepare flags, none of which have been run against a real client yet.  Some of those operations - op_ping and the scrollable cursor operations among them - aren't implemented at all, so a client that negotiates a higher version and then uses one of them has its session ended.  See [firebirdprotolimitations.html Limitations].

Lowering the maximum below 13 turns off the plugin-based login.  Clients log in with the older password hash instead, and wire encryption is unavailable, since only version 13 and up has anywhere to carry the encryption key.  See [firebirdprotowirecrypt.html Wire Encryption].

